<!-- ===== SECTION 1: WHY NUMPY? ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="why-numpy">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>Why NumPy? Arrays vs Lists</h2>

  <p>Python lists are flexible but slow. They store references to objects scattered around memory. When you multiply a list by a number, Python creates a new list with repeated references. NumPy arrays are different: contiguous blocks of homogeneous data in memory. This simplicity makes them orders of magnitude faster for numerical operations. In machine learning, you perform billions of mathematical operations. Using lists instead of arrays would make training a neural network take hours instead of seconds.</p>

  <!-- ANALOGY BOX -->
  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>A Python list is like a scattered collection of boxes with pointers to different locations. Accessing an element requires following the pointer. A NumPy array is like a perfectly organized warehouse with items in consecutive rows. Accessing any item is instant. This organization makes bulk operations on millions of items blazingly fast.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- INFOGRAPHIC 1: Performance Comparison -->
  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Lists vs Arrays: Performance on 1 Million Elements</div>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 800 280" style="width:100%;max-width:800px;margin:0 auto;display:block">
      <!-- List bar -->
      <rect x="50" y="50" width="700" height="35" rx="4" fill="rgba(255,149,0,0.2)" stroke="#C0522F" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="65" y="72" fill="#C0522F" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Python List</text>
      <text x="760" y="72" text-anchor="end" fill="#C0522F" font-size="10" font-weight="600">~0.5 seconds</text>

      <!-- Array bar -->
      <rect x="50" y="100" width="28" height="35" rx="4" fill="rgba(6,255,165,0.2)" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="65" y="122" fill="#16a34a" font-size="10" font-weight="600">NumPy Array</text>
      <text x="760" y="122" text-anchor="end" fill="#16a34a" font-size="10" font-weight="600">~0.01 seconds</text>

      <!-- Speedup annotation -->
      <text x="50" y="180" fill="#1A3A6B" font-size="11" font-weight="600">Performance Gain: ~50x faster</text>
      <text x="50" y="200" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">NumPy handles bulk operations at C speed instead of Python speed</text>
      <text x="50" y="220" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">With millions of data points in ML, this difference multiplies to hours of saved computation</text>
      <text x="50" y="240" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10">Modern GPUs further accelerate NumPy operations for AI workloads</text>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="nm">np</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">time</span>

<span class="cm"># Python list: slow</span>
<span class="va">py_list</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">list</span>(<span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="nm">1_000_000</span>))
<span class="va">start</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">time</span>.<span class="fn">time</span>()
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">x</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">py_list</span>]
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"List: </span>{<span class="nm">time</span>.<span class="fn">time</span>() <span class="op">-</span> <span class="va">start</span>:.4f}s<span class="st">"</span>)

<span class="cm"># NumPy array: blazing fast</span>
<span class="va">np_array</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">arange</span>(<span class="nm">1_000_000</span>)
<span class="va">start</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">time</span>.<span class="fn">time</span>()
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">np_array</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">2</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"NumPy: </span>{<span class="nm">time</span>.<span class="fn">time</span>() <span class="op">-</span> <span class="va">start</span>:.4f}s<span class="st">"</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> List comprehension processes each element with Python interpreter overhead. NumPy's multiplication is vectorized—the entire array operation executes in optimized C code. The speedup often exceeds 50x for large arrays, scaling further with GPUs.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>NumPy arrays are orders of magnitude faster than Python lists for numerical operations</li>
      <li>Speed comes from contiguous memory layout and C-level implementation</li>
      <li>Vectorization means writing operations without explicit loops</li>
      <li>Always use NumPy for any numerical/ML work with substantial data</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

