<!-- ===== SECTION 3: ARRAY PROPERTIES ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="array-properties">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Understanding Array Properties</h2>

  <p>Every array has properties that describe its structure and content. Understanding these is essential for debugging shape mismatches in your code, especially when building neural networks where dimensions must align perfectly.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="nm">np</span>

<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>], [<span class="nm">4</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">6</span>]])

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Shape: </span>{<span class="va">arr</span>.<span class="va">shape</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)        <span class="cm"># (2, 3) — 2 rows, 3 columns</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Dtype: </span>{<span class="va">arr</span>.<span class="va">dtype</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)        <span class="cm"># int64 — data type</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Size: </span>{<span class="va">arr</span>.<span class="va">size</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)         <span class="cm"># 6 — total elements</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Ndim: </span>{<span class="va">arr</span>.<span class="va">ndim</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)         <span class="cm"># 2 — number of dimensions</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Itemsize: </span>{<span class="va">arr</span>.<span class="va">itemsize</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)   <span class="cm"># 8 — bytes per element</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Nbytes: </span>{<span class="va">arr</span>.<span class="va">nbytes</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)     <span class="cm"># 48 — total bytes</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> Shape tells you dimensions. Dtype is crucial—int64 uses more memory than int8, float32 vs float64 affects precision. Size is total elements. These properties matter enormously when building models: the model expects specific input shapes, and mismatches cause cryptic errors.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- WHY THIS MATTERS -->
  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Financial AI:</strong> Trading algorithms must process exact quantities without type confusion. Float64 (double precision) is standard for money calculations to avoid rounding errors on millions of transactions. Knowing dtype helps prevent silent bugs where calculations lose precision.</p>
    <p><strong>In Life Sciences AI:</strong> Medical imaging data is gigabytes large. Understanding nbytes helps you know if data fits in memory. Choosing the right dtype (float32 for images, int16 for some sensors) balances memory and precision for different applications.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>shape: dimensions of the array (crucial for ML)</li>
      <li>dtype: data type of elements (int64, float32, etc.)</li>
      <li>size: total number of elements</li>
      <li>ndim: number of dimensions (1D, 2D, 3D, etc.)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- QUIZ -->
  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>🧪 Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="2">
      <p>An array with shape (3, 4, 5) has how many total elements?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) 12</li>
        <li>B) 3 + 4 + 5 = 12</li>
        <li>C) 3 × 4 × 5 = 60</li>
        <li>D) 35</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">Size is the product of all dimensions: 3 × 4 × 5 = 60. This is the total number of individual elements in the array.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

