<!-- ===== SECTION 4: ELEMENT-WISE OPERATIONS ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="element-wise-ops">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Element-Wise Operations and Vectorization</h2>

  <p>NumPy magic happens with element-wise operations. When you add, multiply, or apply functions to arrays, the operation applies to every element without explicit loops. This is vectorization—the key to fast scientific computing.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="nm">np</span>

<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">4</span>])

<span class="cm"># Arithmetic operations — applied to every element</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">+</span> <span class="nm">10</span>)      <span class="cm"># [11, 12, 13, 14]</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">2</span>)       <span class="cm"># [2, 4, 6, 8]</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">**</span> <span class="nm">2</span>)      <span class="cm"># [1, 4, 9, 16] — squaring</span>

<span class="cm"># Element-wise array operations</span>
<span class="va">arr2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="nm">20</span>, <span class="nm">30</span>, <span class="nm">40</span>])
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">+</span> <span class="va">arr2</span>)    <span class="cm"># [11, 22, 33, 44]</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="va">arr2</span>)    <span class="cm"># [10, 40, 90, 160]</span>

<span class="cm"># Mathematical functions</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">sqrt</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>))     <span class="cm"># Square root of each</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">sin</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>))      <span class="cm"># Sine of each</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">exp</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>))      <span class="cm"># e^x for each</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> Every operation broadcasts to all elements. Add 10 to an array—all elements get 10 added. Multiply two arrays—element-by-element multiplication. No loops, no Python overhead, just fast C code executing on every element simultaneously.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Arithmetic operations apply to every element automatically</li>
      <li>Comparing arrays element-wise returns boolean arrays</li>
      <li>Mathematical functions work on all elements at once</li>
      <li>Vectorization eliminates explicit loops—the key to speed</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ===== SECTION 5: AGGREGATION ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="aggregation">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Aggregation Functions</h2>

  <p>Beyond element-wise operations, you often need to compute summary statistics: sum, mean, max, min. NumPy provides fast aggregation functions that compute across dimensions.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="nm">np</span>

<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>], [<span class="nm">4</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">6</span>]])

<span class="cm"># Global aggregations</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">sum</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>))       <span class="cm"># 21 — sum all</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>))      <span class="cm"># 3.5 — average</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">std</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>))       <span class="cm"># Standard deviation</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">max</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>))       <span class="cm"># 6 — maximum</span>

<span class="cm"># Along specific axes</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">sum</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>, <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0</span>)) <span class="cm"># [5, 7, 9] — sum columns</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">sum</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>, <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>)) <span class="cm"># [6, 15] — sum rows</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>, <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>)) <span class="cm"># [2, 5] — mean per row</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> Aggregations compute single values from arrays. With axis parameter, you specify the dimension to aggregate across. axis=0 collapses rows (sum columns), axis=1 collapses columns (sum rows). This is essential for data science—computing statistics per batch, per sample, per feature.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>sum(), mean(), std(), max(), min() compute summary statistics</li>
      <li>axis parameter controls which dimension to aggregate</li>
      <li>axis=0 aggregates rows (result has same columns)</li>
      <li>axis=1 aggregates columns (result has same rows)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

